    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Field data focused on deformation band and deformation zone structures, showing a dominant shear component, was collected at the Navajo Sandstone outcrops in Capitol wash, Capitol Reef N.P. of Utah. The data was preliminarily analyzed in attempt to identify and evaluate geometric principles governing the deformation architecture. A repetitive pattern referred to as “ladder structure” (Jamison & Stearns 1982, Antonellini & Aydin 1995, Davis 1999) consisting of two sub parallel deformation bands (or zones) segments connected by antithetic segments (“rungs”), was recognized as a common deformation feature and documented in several orders of scale (centimeters to several meters). Preliminarily analysis of the sense of shearing, orientations and spacing of the structure bands and zones has demonstrated power law dependency between the spacing of the rungs and the spacing of their compliant sub parallel segments, and a trend of angle change between the rungs and segments which is consistent with the ladder size. Higher order shear faults (tens of meters long) examined in the Aztec Sandstone, laterally equivalent to the Navajo formation at Valley of Fire State Park, Nevada (Myers &Thompson 1998) show coherence to the geometrical features mentioned. This evidence may suggest common principles governing the architecture of shear faults extending over broad range of scales of scales.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
